# Chapter 3, Edit, the work on the shape of the document: blocks and columns
# (Append, Join), pieces of the document (Copy, Cut, Delete) and sections
# (Contract, Expand and Go To).  The headlines are of the second level and
# below.

.h2 Blocks and Columns [edit-blocks]
.ix Blocks
.ix Block, new
.ix Columns, adding
A document grows by blocks: a block is a paragraph, a headline, a table row
or any other unit of text with a format of its own, and a block is made of
one or more columns side by side ({n:view}).  Enter does not make a new
block; it makes a new line inside the block ({n:edit-typing}).  A
block is made with Append, two blocks are made one with Join, and one block
is made two with Split ({n:edit-b-split}).  Pieces of text, blocks and
sections are moved and copied as described in {x:edit-copy}, and put in by
Paste ({n:edit-b-paste}).
.table 23 37
To do this | Use
Add a column, a block or a section | Append, {k:Alt-F2}, and the keys below.
Make two blocks or two sections one | Join, {k:Alt-F3}.
Split a block or a section in two | Split ({n:edit-b-split}).
.end

.h3 Append [edit-append]
.ix APPEND command
.ix Alt-F2
.ix Ctrl-A
.ix Ctrl-O
.ix Ins
.ix Shift-Ins
.ix Append a column
.ix Append a block
.ix Append a global block
.ix Append a peer section
.ix Append a subsection
.ix Peer section
.ix Subsection
.ix Global-block
.ix Sub-section
.ix Peer-section
.ix New block
.ix New section
.ix Add column
.ix Table list, F8
.ix Block, same local format
.ix Block, global format
.ix No section here
Append adds something new to the document next to the thing that the cursor
is in: a column at the right of a block, a block after the block, a section
after the section, or a subsection inside the section.  The new thing is
empty and the cursor is put in it, ready to type.
.table 22 38
Way | What it does
{k:Alt-F2} | Opens the list Append; choose one of the five.
{k:Ctrl-F}, then {c:Append (Alt-F2)} | The same, for terminals.
{k:Ctrl-A} | Block, without the list.
{k:Ctrl-O} | Global-block, without the list.
{k:Ins} | Peer-section, without the list.
{k:Shift-Ins} | Sub-section, without the list.
{k:F8}, then Add column | Column, without the list.
.end
Append is on the {k:F12} menu bar ({k:F12}, Edit, Parts, Append), and it has no
panel of its own.  When a terminal does
not hand {k:Alt-F2} to the Editor, the {k:Ctrl-F} list works everywhere.  Most
terminals use {k:Shift-Ins} to paste the clipboard of the desktop and never
hand it to the Editor: there, append a subsection from the list
({k:Alt-F2}, then {k:S}).
.pic pictures/edit-a-append.png 5 The list Append in a structured document.
.h4 The list Append
The list is a pop-up list ({n:edit-popups}).  It opens with Block selected.
The choices, in order:
.table 12 48
Choice | What is appended
Column | A column at the right of the block of the cursor.
Block | A block after this block, with the format of this block.
Global-block | A block after this block, with the global format.
Peer-section | A section after this section, at its level.
Sub-section | A section inside this section, at the next level.
.end
In an unstructured document the list has three choices, Column, Block and
Global-block.  A letter chooses at once: {k:C}, {k:B}, {k:G}, {k:P}, {k:S}.
{k:Esc} closes the list and nothing is appended.
.h4 Column
Append, Column adds an empty column at the right edge of the block.  The
block then has one more column, and the cursor stands at its start.
.table 21 39
Detail | What happens
Width of the new column | None of its own: it takes what is left.
Width of the old last column | Set, if it had none (see below).
Text of the new column | One empty line.
Format of the new column | The global format (no local format).
Cursor | At the start of the new column.
.end
A column that is last has no width: it extends to the right edge.  When
another column is appended, the old last column gets the width of the text
width divided by the new number of columns, in positions.  With the text width
of 80, a block of one column that gets a second is cut into a column of 40
positions and the new one, which takes the other 40; a third column
makes the middle one 26 positions wide.  The widths can be changed afterwards
with the format of the column ({n:format}).  Appending a column to a block
whose last column already has a width leaves the widths alone and adds the
new column with no width.
.pic pictures/edit-a-blocks.png 5 A block made of two columns by Append, Column and typed into.
The ruler follows the cursor into the new column, which is how the width and
the tab stops of each column can be seen.  Columns are the way to make
tables, lists in two parts, and labels that sit beside text.
.h4 Block
Append, Block adds an empty block after the block of the cursor, in the same
section, and puts the cursor in it.  The new block has the local format of
the block that it follows: the block formats, the number and the widths of
the columns, and the formats of every column are copied; the text is not.
Appending to a row of a table is therefore the way to make the next row.
{k:Ctrl-A} does this without the list.  The new block is put directly after
the block of the cursor, before any other block that follows.
.table 25 35
The cursor is in | The new block has
A block of text | The format of that block.
A headline | The global format (the headline format is not copied).
A block of several columns | The same columns, empty.
.end
.h4 Global-block
Append, Global-block adds an empty block of one column after the block of
the cursor, with no local format: the format of the section or of the
document applies, which is the format that Global, Format sets.  {k:Ctrl-O}
does this without the list.  Use it when the block that holds the cursor has
a special format (a hanging indent, a centered line, a table row) that the
next block should not have.
.h4 Peer-section
Append, Peer-section adds a new section after the section of the cursor, at
the same level.  If the cursor is in section 2.2, the new section is 2.3
(the old 2.3 becomes 2.4); if 2.2 has subsections, they stay with it and
the new section follows them.  The new section has one empty block, its
headline, and the cursor stands in it.  {k:Ins} does this without the list.
.pic pictures/edit-a-fold-1.png 5 An outline of the sample document.  A peer section can be appended while the outline is shown.
.h4 Sub-section
Append, Sub-section adds a new section inside the section of the cursor, one
level lower.  It is put after the subsections that already exist: it
becomes the last, and if the section had none, the first and only
subsection.  The cursor stands in the new headline.  {k:Shift-Ins} does this
without the list.
.table 23 37
Before (cursor in 2.2) | After Sub-section
2.2 with no subsection | 2.2 has the subsection 2.2.1
2.2 with 2.2.1 and 2.2.2 | 2.2 has also 2.2.3
2.2 followed by 2.3 | The new section is 2.2.1; 2.3 stays 2.3
.end
The section number in the status line changes at once to that of the new
section, and the document is marked as changed (the asterisk).  Text that
is typed into a new headline is the title of the new section.
.h4 In an unstructured document
{k:Ins} and {k:Shift-Ins} append a block of the global format, like
{k:Ctrl-O}.  The same keys do the same inside the editors of a note, a
comment and an equation; there {k:Ins} saves the text instead ({n:edit-modes}).
.h4 Notes on Append
- A block that was added by mistake is removed with {k:Del} while it is
  empty ({n:edit-deleting}), a section with {k:Ctrl-L}, a column by
  moving the text out of it.
- With the cursor in no section (in the text before the first headline) {k:Ins}
  and {k:Shift-Ins} give the message "No section here".
- While an area is highlighted for Copy, Cut, Move or Delete the Append
  keys are refused ({n:edit-highlight}).
- A document that has been collapsed ({n:edit-contract}) can be
  appended to: the new section is shown, the folded ones stay folded.
- The Table list of {k:F8} has two choices: Add column does what Append,
  Column does, and Split column here does what Split, Column does ({n:edit-b-split}).
.h4 Examples
To add a section of the same level after 2.2, with its text:
1. Put the cursor anywhere in section 2.2 (its headline, its text or its
   subsections).
2. Press {k:Ins}.  The cursor is in the empty headline of the new section.
3. Type the headline.  Press {k:Ctrl-A} to add a block for the text of the
   section, and type the text.
To make a table of two columns and three rows:
1. In an empty block press {k:Alt-F2}, {k:C}.  The block has two columns.
2. Type the first cell, press {k:Right} to go to the second column, type
   it, and press {k:Ctrl-A}: the new block has two columns, like the first.
3. Repeat from step 2 for each of the other rows.

.h3 Join [edit-join]
.ix JOIN command
.ix Alt-F3
.ix Join blocks
.ix Join sections
.ix Join, Block
.ix Join, Section
.ix Cannot join here
.ix Cannot join a section that has subsections
.ix No following peer section
.ix Join works on single-column blocks
.ix Nothing to join here
.ix Merge two blocks
Join makes one of two neighbors: two blocks become one block, or two
sections become one section.  It is the opposite of Split ({n:edit-b-split}).  The first of the two is the one that holds the cursor,
and what is joined to it is what follows.
.table 23 37
Way | What it does
{k:Alt-F3} | The list Join: Block, Section.
{k:Ctrl-F}, then {c:Join (Alt-F3)} | The same, for terminals.
{k:Del} | Join, Block, at the end of a column ({n:edit-deleting}).
{k:Backspace} | Join, Block, at the start of a block.
.end
.pic pictures/edit-a-join-menu.png 5 The list Join in a structured document.
The list has two choices, Block and Section, and in an unstructured document
one, Block.  The key {k:B} or {k:S} chooses at once; {k:Esc} cancels.  Join
is on the {k:F12} menu bar: {k:F12}, Edit, Parts, Join.
.h4 Block
Join, Block joins the block of the cursor and the block that follows it
in the same section.
.table 16 44
Detail | What happens
The text | The next block's text follows the text of this one.
The end of line | The end-of-line of the first block is dropped.
The format | The block keeps its own format; the other is lost.
The cursor | At the place where the two texts meet.
The other block | Removed from the document.
.end
The two blocks join end to end with no space put between them: if the text
of the first block ends with "Sales" and the next begins with "Sales grew",
the result is "SalesSales grew".  If the first block was a headline, the text
of the next block becomes part of the headline, which is how a headline made
from two lines is closed up.  A joined block that holds markers keeps them.

Both blocks must have one column.  The message for blocks of several columns
is "Join works on single-column blocks" and nothing is joined.  When the
cursor is in the last block of a section, there is no block that follows (a
section, a subsection or the end of the document follows), and the message is
"Cannot join here".  The blocks of two sections are not joined by this choice;
use Join, Section.
.h4 Section
Join, Section joins the section of the cursor and the following section
at the same level: the second becomes part of the first.  The headline of
the second section becomes an ordinary block, with the text of the first;
its blocks follow; its subsections become subsections of the first.
.table 24 36
Before (cursor in 2.2) | After Join, Section
2.2 Costs, with text | 2.2 Costs, text, a block "People" and its text
2.3 People, with text |  (the headline of 2.3 is now text)
.end
.pic pictures/edit-a-fold-minus.png 5 After the sections are contracted, the sections to join can be seen together.
The numbers of the sections that follow go down by one.  The headline block
of the second section keeps its format but is no longer a headline, so it is
printed as text.  The cursor stays in the first section.
.table 28 30
Message | When
Cannot join a section that has subsections | The first has subsections.
No following peer section | There is no section after.
.end
The first message is given when the first section has subsections: they would
be left between its text and the text of the second.  Remove or move them
first (Cut and Paste), or join the sections from the last backward.  The
second message is given when the first section is the last at its level; the
next section of the parent is not a peer.
.h4 Notes on Join
- The joined block takes the format of the first.  If the second block
  was a table row, its columns are lost.
- Split ({n:edit-b-split}) with the cursor at the junction makes two blocks
  again, but not the format of the second.
- {k:Backspace} at the start of a headline does nothing.
- Autosort ({n:edit-b-sort}) works on blocks and sections; it does not join
  them.
.h4 Example
A paragraph that was split in two by Enter and {k:Ctrl-V} is made one again:
put the cursor at the end of the first block and press {k:Alt-F3}, {k:B}; or
press {k:Del} at its end.  The cursor stands between the two parts.

.h2 Copy, Cut and Delete [edit-copy]
.ix COPY command
.ix CUT command
.ix DELETE command
.ix F3
.ix F4
.ix Alt-F4
.ix Ctrl-X
.ix Ctrl-K
.ix Ctrl-L
.ix Copy text
.ix Copy a column
.ix Copy a block
.ix Copy a section
.ix Cut text
.ix Cut a column
.ix Cut a block
.ix Cut a section
.ix Delete text
.ix Delete a column
.ix Delete a block
.ix Delete a section
.ix Clipboard
.ix Highlight
.ix Copied
.ix Deleted
.ix Nothing to paste
.ix Text selection must stay within one column
.ix Block selection must stay within one section
.ix Section selection must cover peer sections
.ix Cannot take the whole document as a section
.ix Sections exist only in structured documents
Three commands take a piece of the document.  Copy makes a copy of it in the
clipboard, and the document is not changed.  Cut removes it and keeps it in
the clipboard.  Delete removes it and keeps nothing: it is for a piece that
is not wanted again, and the clipboard keeps what it had.  Paste
({n:edit-b-paste}) puts the contents of the clipboard anywhere, any number of
times.  The three work in the same way: a list of four choices, a highlight
that shows the piece, {k:Enter} to do it and {k:Esc} to cancel.  The piece can
be a stretch of text, a column, a block or a section.

Delete here is the Delete of the edit screen.  Delete in the File menu removes a file
from the disk ({n:file}), Delete in Tools, Macros and Tools, Library removes a
macro or an entry, and Delete in Global, Named removes a named block format.
The keys that delete without a highlight ({k:Del}, {k:Backspace},
{k:Ctrl-D}, {k:Alt-Backspace}) are in {x:edit-deleting}.

.h3 How to reach the commands
.table 23 37
Way | What it does
{k:F3} | The list Copy; choose Text, Column, Block or Section.
{k:F4} | The list Cut: the same four choices.
{k:Alt-F4} | The list Delete: the same four choices.
{k:Ctrl-F}, then {c:Delete (Alt-F4)} | The same, for terminals.
{k:Ctrl-X} | Starts a Text highlight at once; {k:Enter} cuts it.
{k:Ctrl-K} | Cuts the block of the cursor at once, no highlight.
{k:Ctrl-L} | Cuts the section of the cursor at once, no highlight.
{k:Ctrl-F3}, {k:Ctrl-F4} | Lib Copy and Lib Cut: as Copy and Cut, and the
 library as well (below).
.end
None of them is on the {k:F12} menu bar.  Many window managers close the window on
{k:Alt-F4}; if the Editor does not get the key, use the {k:Ctrl-F} list.
.pic pictures/edit-a-copy-menu.png 5 The list Copy: Text, Column, Block and Section.  The lists of Cut and Delete look alike; the title says which.

.h3 The steps
1. Press {k:F3}, {k:F4} or {k:Alt-F4}.  A list shows four choices: Text,
   Column, Block and, in a structured document only, Section.  Choose with
   {k:Up}, {k:Down} and {k:Return}, or press the first letter ({k:T},
   {k:C}, {k:B}, {k:S}), which chooses at once.  {k:Esc} closes the list.
2. The message line shows "Highlight the area with the cursor keys, then press
   Enter (Esc cancels)".  The area starts at the cursor and is drawn in
   the colors of the highlight.  Move the cursor to make the area larger
   or smaller.
3. Press {k:Enter}.  The area is copied, cut or deleted, and the message is
   "Copied", "Cut" or "Deleted".  {k:Esc} instead cancels: the message is
   "Cancelled" and nothing is done.
Look at what is highlighted before {k:Enter}: none of the three prompts for
confirmation.

.h3 What the choices take
.table 8 52
Choice | The area
Text | From where the cursor was to where it is, within one column.
Column | The whole text of the column that holds the cursor.
Block | The blocks from the first block to the one the cursor is in.
Section | The sections from the first one to the one the cursor is in.
.end
.table 9 23 24
Choice | Cut and Delete remove | What stays
Text | The characters; the text closes up. | The block.
Column | The text of the column. | The column, empty.
Block | The blocks of the area. | One block per section.
Section | The sections and their subsections. | A section 1, if empty.
.end
.h4 Text
The area is the characters between the place where the cursor was when
Text was chosen (the anchor) and the place where it is now; the anchor may
be before or after the cursor.  The area can contain tabs, line ends,
attribute changes and markers.  It must stay in one column of one block.
If the cursor ends in another block or column, {k:Enter} gives the message
"Text selection must stay within one column" and nothing is done; the
highlight is gone and the command must be started again.  Choosing Text and
pressing {k:Enter} at once copies nothing, and that empty copy replaces what
the clipboard had.  Cut and Delete close up the text that followed the area,
and the cursor is put at the place where the text was.
.pic pictures/edit-a-highlight.png 5 Copy, Text: from the start of the paragraph to the cursor.
.h4 Column
The area is all the text of the column that holds the cursor; it does not
grow, so press {k:Enter} at once.  The clipboard holds the text, not the
column: Paste puts the text in at the cursor like Text.  Cut and Delete leave
the column in the block with one empty line.
.h4 Block
.pic pictures/edit-a-highlight-block.png 5 Copy, Block: the area covers whole blocks, from the start to the cursor.
The area is whole blocks: all the blocks from the one where the cursor was to
the one where it is now, and the movement keys make it longer or shorter a
block at a time.  A block that is partly in the area is wholly in it.  All
the blocks must be in one section: when the area crosses the start of a
section the message is "Block selection must stay within one section".  A
copied block keeps its format, its columns and its text.  Paste puts the
blocks after the block of the cursor.

A section must keep one block: when all the blocks of a section are cut or
deleted, a new empty block is put in the section and the cursor stands in it.
If the headline was among the blocks, the first block that follows it becomes
the headline.  After a cut the cursor is at the end of the block above.
Cutting a block of a column, as part of a table, removes the row; Paste puts
the row in after another row.
.h4 Section
In a structured document, the area is whole sections, each with its
subsections.  The area starts in the section where the cursor was and
ends in the section where it is now; both must be peers (at the same level
and with the same parent), otherwise the message is "Section selection must
cover peer sections".  The top of the document cannot be taken: "Cannot take
the whole document as a section".  Choosing Section and pressing {k:Enter} at
once takes the section of the cursor and its subsections.  An unstructured
document has no Section choice.  Paste puts the copy after the section of the
cursor, at its level, after its subsections.

Cut and Delete take the sections out as one piece, and the numbers of the
sections that follow are made again.  The last section of the document cannot
disappear: when the only top-level section is removed, an empty section 1
takes its place.  The cursor is put at the end of the section above.  A
section that is contracted is taken whole, with its text.
.table 28 30
Message | When
Block selection must stay within one section | The blocks cross a section.
Section selection must cover peer sections | The sections are not peers.
Cannot take the whole document as a section | The root was chosen.
Sections exist only in structured documents | A section, in unstructured.
Text selection must stay within one column | The text crosses a column.
.end

.h3 The highlight [edit-highlight]
The cursor keys move the cursor and change the area.  {k:Enter} accepts,
{k:Esc} cancels, {k:F1} is Help, and every other key is refused.  Append, the
keys that contract and expand, and the other commands that change the
document cannot be used while an area is highlighted.

.h3 The clipboard
.ix Clipboard, one at a time
The clipboard holds one thing: the last Copy, Cut or Move.  A second Copy
replaces the first, and Paste does not empty it, so the same copy can be
pasted again and again.  Delete leaves the clipboard alone.  The clipboard
is the memory of the Editor: it is lost when the Editor ends, it is not
the clipboard of the desktop and it is not shared with other programs.
Retrieving or beginning another document does not clear it, so a Copy in
one document can be pasted in another.  Paste with nothing in the clipboard
shows "Nothing to paste".

Copy never changes the document: the asterisk of the status line is not put
up by it.  Cut and Delete do change it.  A marker is one item of the text, so
it is carried with the text, and when it is copied into another place it is
the same marker (a counter is still the counter that it was, a note has the
same text).  Block and Section copies keep the markers, the formats and the
state of the sections that they hold.

.h3 Cut [edit-cut]
Cut is Copy followed by the removal of the area.  Three keys cut without the
list: {k:Ctrl-K} cuts the block that holds the cursor, without asking, and
{k:Ctrl-L} cuts the section that holds the cursor with its subsections;
{k:Ctrl-X} starts the cutting of text.  They are the quick form of {k:F4}, Block,
{k:Enter} and {k:F4}, Section, {k:Enter} with the area limited to the one block or
the one section.  {k:Ctrl-L} does nothing in an unstructured document and when
the section is the whole document.  The cut is in the clipboard, so a cut by
mistake is put back with {k:F5} at once; the section or block is put after the
block or section of the cursor.
.pic pictures/edit-a-flat.png 5 A short unstructured document.  Ctrl-K cuts the whole block under the cursor.
An area that is cut and not pasted, followed by another Cut or Copy, is lost.
Move ({k:F7}, {n:edit-b-move}) does not use the clipboard: it moves the area
in the place.
.caution Cut keeps the piece only until the next Copy or Cut, and Delete keeps
nothing: after {k:Enter} a deleted area is gone.  When there is a doubt, use
Cut, because the clipboard holds the piece.

.h3 Delete [edit-delete]
Delete removes the area and does not touch the clipboard, even when it held
nothing.  A hidden (contracted) section is deleted with its text.  Delete has
no key without a list, like {k:Ctrl-K}.

.h3 Lib Copy, Lib Cut and the library
{k:Ctrl-F3} and {k:Ctrl-F4} do Copy and Cut and, in addition, add the area to
the library of the Tools menu under a name that a panel prompts for
({n:edit-c-library-keys}).  The library keeps the entry after the Editor ends.

.h3 Examples
To repeat a paragraph in another section: put the cursor in the paragraph,
press {k:F3}, {k:B}, {k:Enter} (the area is the one block); move to the
other section, put the cursor in the block that the copy is to follow, and
press {k:F5}.

To move a section of the report to the end of the document: put the cursor
in the section and press {k:Ctrl-L}; press {k:Shift-End} to go to the end of
the document and {k:F5}.  The section is put after the section the cursor is
in (the last one), at the level of the last section.

.h2 Sections: Contract, Expand and Go To [edit-sections]
.ix Sections, working with
A long structured document is read and changed one section at a time.  This
part describes the commands that show less of the document, so that its
headlines can be read, and the command that goes to a section by its number.
The commands that make sections are Append and Join (above) and Split
({n:edit-b-split}); the commands that show a whole level at once, and move a
section to another level, are in {x:edit-b-outlines} and {x:edit-b-move}.

.h3 Contract and Expand [edit-contract]
.ix CONTRACT and EXPAND
.ix Contract
.ix Expand
.ix Collapse a section
.ix Expand a section
.ix Alt--
.ix Alt-+
.ix Alt-*
.ix Alt-=
.ix Keypad -
.ix Keypad +
.ix Keypad *
.ix Fold
.ix Fold state
.ix Section marks
.ix Fold mark
.ix Hide text
.ix Collapse/expand works in structured documents
.ix Plus mark
.ix Star mark
.ix Hidden sections
Contract and Expand hide and show the parts of a structured document so
that its headlines can be read without the text.  A section that is
contracted keeps its headline; its text and its subsections are out of
sight (not removed) until it is expanded.  Three keys do it, one section
at a time; the keys that show a whole level at once, {k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9} and
{k:Alt-0}, are in {x:edit-b-outlines}.
.pic pictures/edit-a-fold-minus.png 5 Section 2.1 contracted with Alt--: its headline stays, a + stands in the rule above it, and its text and subsections are out of sight.
.table 17 43
Key | What it does
{k:Alt--} | Contract the current section to its headline.
{k:Alt-+}, {k:Alt-=} | Expand the current section by one level.
{k:Alt-*} | Hide, or show, the text of the section.
.end
The commands are keys; they are not on the {k:F12} menu bar and have no panel.
In the X11 version the keys of the numeric keypad with a minus, a plus and an
asterisk work as {k:Alt--}, {k:Alt-+} and {k:Alt-*}.  A terminal sends those
keys as the plain characters, which would be typed into the text, so in a
terminal only the Alt keys work; the {k:Alt-=} key is the {k:Alt-+} key
without Shift.  The keys work in a structured document only: in an
unstructured one the message line shows "Collapse/expand works in structured
documents".  The keys are refused while an area is highlighted
({n:edit-highlight}).
.h4 The four states of a section
Every section is in one of four states.  The state is saved with the
document, so a document that was left contracted is contracted when it is
retrieved again.
.table 15 6 38
State | Mark | What is shown of the section
Open | none | The headline, the text and the subsections.
Text hidden | * | The headline and the subsections; not the text.
Collapsed | + | The headline only.
Headline and text | + | The headline and the text; not the subsections.
.end
The fourth state is made by the keys {k:Alt-1} to {k:Alt-9} ({n:edit-b-outlines}); the three keys of this section make the first three.
The state of a section is shown in the double rule above its headline: a +
or a * in bold, three positions from the left edge of the rule's own indent.
A section that is open has no mark.  A section that has no subsections and
is marked + was collapsed only to hide its text.
.pic pictures/edit-a-fold-1.png 5 The whole document shown as headlines (Alt-1): the + marks are in the rules.
.h4 Contract: Alt--
{k:Alt--} makes the current section Collapsed: the mark + stands in the rule
above its headline, and its text and all its subsections go out of sight.
The current section is the one that holds the cursor; in a document whose
sections have subsections it is the innermost, so the key on a block of
2.1.2 collapses 2.1.2 and leaves 2.1 as it was.  The cursor goes to the
start of the headline, because the block it stood in has gone out of
sight.  {k:Alt--} on a section that is already collapsed changes nothing.
The key marks the document as changed.
.h4 Expand: Alt-+
{k:Alt-+} expands the current section by one level, which makes it
possible to open an outline step by step:
.table 24 36
The section is | {k:Alt-+} makes
Collapsed or with text hidden | Open; each direct subsection is collapsed
Open | Its direct subsections open, theirs collapsed
.end
After the first press of {k:Alt-+} on the section 2 of the sample document
that was collapsed, the text of 2 is shown, and so are the headlines of
2.1, 2.2 and 2.3, each with the mark + (their text and their subsections are
hidden).  After the second press, 2.1, 2.2 and 2.3 are open and 2.1.1 and
2.1.2 show as headlines with the mark +.  Each press shows one more level
of the document under the section, and a section that is not collapsed
never shows less.  A subsection that has no subsections of its own is
opened.  The cursor stays where it is: Expand never hides the
block that holds the cursor.
.h4 Hide the text: Alt-*
{k:Alt-*} hides only the text of the section (all its blocks but the
headline): the mark * is set and the subsections stay in sight.  Pressed
again on a section that has the mark *, it shows the text again.  Pressed on
a collapsed section (mark +) it opens the section in full.  Use it to read
the headlines of a section and of its subsections without the paragraphs
between them.  If the cursor was in the text that is hidden, it goes to the
headline.
.table 16 16 24
The section is | {k:Alt-*} makes | The mark
Open | Text hidden | *
Text hidden | Open | none
Collapsed | Open | none
.end
.h4 The cursor and what is hidden
The cursor is always in a block that can be seen.  When a key hides the
block that holds it, the cursor moves to the headline of the section.  The
movement keys skip hidden blocks: {k:Down} on a collapsed headline goes to
the headline that follows, and the section keys ({k:Shift-Down},
{k:Shift-PgDn}) do the same.
.h4 What hidden text takes part in
Hidden text is still part of the document.
.table 23 37
Command | On hidden text
Save, Export, Print | The whole document, hidden or not.
Copy, Cut, Move of a section | The hidden text goes with it.
Search | Opens the sections that hold a match.
Replace | Changes hidden text too.
Autosort, Count | Take hidden text into account.
.end
.h4 Examples
To see the shape of a long report, press {k:Alt-1}: only the top level
headlines remain, each with a +.  To read the contents of Part 2, put the
cursor in it and press {k:Alt-+} once: the headlines of its sections show.
To work on one section of that part, put the cursor in it and press
{k:Alt-+} again.  To put everything back, press {k:Alt-0} twice or
{k:Alt-+} on each section.
.caution A collapsed or contracted section is saved as it is.  A reader who
retrieves the document sees it contracted.  Open the sections that are to be
read before the document is sent away.

.h3 Go To [edit-goto]
.ix GO TO command
.ix Alt-F7
.ix Go To a section
.ix Go To Section
.ix Document start
.ix Document end
.ix Next section
.ix Previous section
.ix Section number
.ix No section
.ix Section number prompt
Go To moves the cursor a long way in one step: to the start or the end of
the document, to the next or the previous section, or to a section by its
number.  It is the key to use in a long structured document, where the
cursor keys would take many presses.
.table 28 30
Way | What it does
{k:Alt-F7} | The list Go To.
{k:Ctrl-F}, then {c:Go To (Alt-F7)} | The same, for terminals.
.end
.pic pictures/edit-a-goto-menu.png 5 The list Go To.
The list is a pop-up list ({n:edit-popups}); it opens with the first
choice selected.
.table 17 43
Choice | Where the cursor goes
Document start | The first character of the first block.
Document end | The end of the last block.
Section number... | The headline of the section whose number is typed.
Next section | The headline of the next section.
Previous section | The headline of the section, or of the one before.
.end
A letter chooses at once, but the letter looks for the next entry after the
selected one.  The first entry is selected when the list opens; so {k:D} does
not choose Document start but Document end; to choose
Document start press {k:Return} or {k:Home}.  The letters {k:S}, {k:N} and
{k:P} choose Section number, Next section and Previous section.
.h4 Document start and Document end
Document start puts the cursor on the first character of the first block that
can be seen; Document end puts it after the last character of the last
visible block.  They are the same as {k:Shift-Home} and {k:Shift-End}.
.h4 Next section and Previous section
Next section puts the cursor on the first character of the headline of the
next section in the text, whatever its level; at the last section it stays
where it is.  It is the same as {k:Shift-Down}.  Previous section goes to
the headline of the section that holds the cursor, or, if the cursor is
already on that headline, to the headline of the section before it, which
is the same as {k:Shift-Up}.  Sections that are hidden by Contract or
Section edit are passed over.  In an unstructured document, Previous
section goes to the start of the document and Next section does nothing.
.h4 Section number
Section number... opens a prompt ({n:edit-prompts}) titled Go To
Section, with the line "Section number (e.g. 2.1):" and a field of dots.
Type the number as the status line shows it, 2, 2.1 or 2.1.2, and press
{k:Enter}; {k:Esc} cancels.
.pic pictures/edit-a-goto-panel.png 5 The prompt of Go To, Section number: the number 2.1.2 is typed.
.table 14 46
Field | Values
Section number | Digits and periods as the status line shows them: 3, 3.2, 2.1.2.
.end
The cursor goes to the first character of the headline of that section.  The
number must be written exactly: "2.1" and "2.1.0" are different, a leading
zero does not count as the same number, and there are no spaces.  The field
is empty each time the prompt opens.
.pic pictures/edit-a-goto-done.png 5 After Enter: the cursor is on the headline of section 2.1.2.
.table 14 36
Message | When
No section 9 | There is no section of that number.
No section | Enter with an empty field.
.end
The message is shown on the message line and the cursor stays where it was.
In an unstructured document the whole document is section 1, so only the
number 1 is found.
.h4 Notes on Go To
- A section that is hidden by Contract or by an outline can be named: the
  status line then shows its number, and the cursor is in text that is
  not drawn.  The next movement key brings the cursor to the nearest
  visible block.  Expand the section first if you mean to work in it.
- Go To does not change the document and does not touch the clipboard.
- The section numbers are made from the position of the sections, so Go To
  after sections have been appended, cut or pasted uses the new numbers.
- The section keys {k:Shift-Up}, {k:Shift-Down}, {k:Shift-PgUp} and
  {k:Shift-PgDn} do the work of Previous and Next section from the keyboard
  directly ({n:edit-moving}).
- Search finds text, and Go To finds a place by its number; Search moves the
  cursor to the match, and in a collapsed document it opens the section
  ({n:edit-b-search}).
.h4 Example
In a report of 40 sections, to go to 3.2.1: press {k:Alt-F7}, {k:S}, type
{c:3.2.1}, press {k:Enter}.  To return to the start: {k:Alt-F7}, {k:Return}.
